{{also|arpeggiò}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|arpeggio}}, from {{m|it|arpeggiare||to play a [[harp]]}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ɑɹˈpɛ.dʒi.oʊ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|arpeggi}}

# {{lb|en|music}} The notes of a chord played individually instead of simultaneously, usually moving from lowest to highest.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1956|author=[[w:Delano Ames|Delano Ames]]|title=Crime out of Mind|chapter=14|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5915292W|passage=He struck the opening chords of the passage; but this time Irene's voice was silent. Victor stopped in the middle of an '''arpeggio'''.}}

